          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Arithmetic topology
            is a theory describing some surprising
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogies"} analogies
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/low-dimensional+topology"} 3-dimensional topology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+theory"} number theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic"}> arithmetic
            ), where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots"} knots
            embedded in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-manifold"} 3-manifold
            behave like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+ideals"} prime ideals
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+of+algebraic+integers"} ring of algebraic integers
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Spec%28Z%29#As3dSpaceContainingKnots"} Spec(Z) – As a 3d space containing knots
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/low-dimensional+topology"} low-dimensional topology
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#GGW20"} GGW20
          %p
            Under the original analogy, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-sphere"} 3-sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
            corresponds to the ring of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi ℚ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} spec(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}})
            (i.e.,
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} spec(\mathbb{Z})
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branched+cover"} unbranched
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            has no non-trivial
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unramified+extensions"} unramified extensions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linking+number"} linking number
            between two embedded knots in the 3-sphere then corresponds to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Legendre+symbol"} Legendre symbol
            between two primes in the ordinary integers.
          %p
            The so-called
            %em M^2KR dictionary
            (Mazur-Morishita-Kapranov-Reznikov) relates terms from each side of the analogy (see sec 2.2 of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Sikora"} Sikora
